# Project conventions

Stack: MERN (MongoDB/Mongoose, Express, React, Node) + client-side TensorFlow.js/MediaPipe for pose estimation. Free-tier only.

**Schema (see docs/decisions.md for full reasoning):**
- Reference `User` from `Workout`, `MealLog`, `FormFeedback` via `userId` — never embed these inside `User`.
- Embed `sets` directly inside `Workout` documents (small, bounded, always read together).
- Stats/aggregation: compute on read via MongoDB aggregation pipelines. Do not add precomputed counter fields to `User`.
- `FormFeedback` stores only detected issues + timestamp. Never store raw video or frame data.

**Auth:** JWT via `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header, verified in `middleware/auth.js`. Never store per-request user data in module-level variables — always scope to `req.user`.

**Code style:** Readable over clever. Every non-obvious line gets a short "why" comment, not a restatement of the code. Flag any implementation detail that isn't simple enough to justify and explain in code review.

**Mongoose gotchas (found in Milestone 2, apply project-wide):**
- Aggregation `$match` stages do NOT auto-cast types the way `.find()`/`.findOne()` do. Always explicitly cast ObjectId fields: `new mongoose.Types.ObjectId(idString)`.
- When sorting on a field that can tie (e.g. a date with no time component), add `_id: 1` as a secondary sort key for deterministic order — MongoDB does not guarantee order among tied documents otherwise.

**Frontend conventions (Milestone 3):**
- JWT is stored in `localStorage`, attached to every request via an axios interceptor (`api/axios.js`) — never attach it manually per-call.
- `ProtectedRoute` is a UX convenience only, not a security boundary. Every backend route must independently filter by `req.user.id` — never assume the frontend check is sufficient.
- React clears DOM refs to `null` synchronously on unmount, BEFORE `useEffect` cleanup functions run. Never rely on a DOM ref (e.g. a `<video>` ref) still being populated inside that same component's unmount cleanup — store any value that must survive to cleanup (like a MediaStream) in its own separate ref instead.

**Real-time / sensor-like data conventions (Milestone 4):**
- Any code that can throw and runs synchronously inside a `useEffect` body needs a readiness guard and/or try/catch — an uncaught error inside an effect crashes the ENTIRE React tree, not just that component, since no error boundary exists in this app.
- Continuous real-world signals (pose landmarks, sensor readings) are noisy frame-to-frame. Smooth with a moving average before feeding into any discrete state machine or threshold check — comparing raw consecutive readings amplifies noise into false transitions.
- Discrete events derived from a noisy continuous signal need explicit debouncing (a minimum time between events, and/or a stricter "confirm" threshold separate from the "reset" threshold) — a single clean threshold alone is not enough to prevent false triggers from jitter/sway.
- Squat form-check constants (tuned against real test data, not defaults): depth threshold 95°, standing threshold 160°, descent-confirm threshold 140°, min rep duration 800ms.
- Never hardcode a fixed body side (left/right) for pose landmarks — filming from the side, one side is naturally occluded and less reliable. Use MediaPipe's per-landmark `visibility` score to dynamically pick whichever side is actually visible each frame. Relevant to any future exercise (e.g. push-up).

**Data retention:**
- Collections with no long-horizon read pattern (e.g. FormFeedback) use a MongoDB TTL index (`expires: '30d'` on the Date field) to auto-delete old documents — zero app code needed. Extends the lean-storage philosophy; apply the same pattern to any future collection with similar characteristics (high write volume, no need for indefinite retention). Do NOT apply this reflexively to every collection — verify actual write volume and long-term value first (MealLog and Workouts deliberately have no TTL; run the storage math before assuming a pattern transfers).

**External API integration (Milestone 5 — Gemini Vision):**
- Any external API with a rate limit shared across the whole project (not per-user) needs explicit handling — treat 429s as a real, expected scenario when multiple users share one API key, not an edge case.
- If a service offers multiple models/tiers with separate quotas, pool them via a fallback chain rather than relying on just one — check the LIVE usage dashboard for actual current limits before assuming documentation or blog posts are accurate.
- Never reuse a working pattern (e.g. a page-reload-on-success callback) in a new component without re-checking whether the original reasoning still applies — the same code can be correct in one place and a bug in another.

**Deployment (Milestone 5 — Render + Vercel):**
- Any SPA using client-side routing (React Router) needs an explicit rewrite rule on the static host (`vercel.json`: rewrite all paths to `/index.html`) or page refresh on nested routes 404s. Near-universal requirement, not an edge case.
- CORS origin matching is exact-match on the `Origin` header — no trailing slash, http vs https must match precisely between the env var and the real deployed URL.
- Vite env vars exposed to client code must be prefixed `VITE_`, and are baked in at BUILD time — changing one after a deploy requires a fresh build, not just a restart.
- Match backend hosting region to the DATABASE's region, not just user proximity — the backend-to-database round trip happens on every request.

**Third-party library integration (Milestone 6 — found via the barcode scanner):**
- Never pass an `async` callback to a third-party library that invokes it inside its own `try/catch`. The rejection escapes that catch, becomes an unhandled promise rejection, and every statement after the throwing line is silently skipped — the library keeps running as if nothing happened. Keep such callbacks synchronous and handle errors inline; fire off async work without awaiting it.
- Verify a method exists in the INSTALLED version before using it (grep `node_modules`, don't trust tutorials or older docs). `@zxing/browser` v0.2.x removed `reset()` — scans are stopped via the `IScannerControls` object returned by `decodeFromConstraints`.
- When a library hands controls to BOTH a callback parameter and a returned promise, prefer the callback's copy — ZXing runs its first decode iteration synchronously, before the promise resolves, so a ref assigned from the return value can still be `null`.
- Per-frame console noise from a scanning/polling library is usually normal operation, not the bug. Confirm what a message actually means before chasing it — the loudest signal is often the least relevant one.

**Camera / secure-context conventions:**
- `getUserMedia` requires a secure context (HTTPS or `localhost`). Testing camera features on a phone via `vite --host` and a LAN IP CANNOT work — the camera never opens, which looks like a broken feature rather than a transport problem. Test against the deployed HTTPS build or an HTTPS tunnel.
- Laptop webcams are fixed-focus and low-resolution; a camera feature failing on desktop but working on a phone is expected, not a code bug. Always request resolution explicitly (`width/height: { ideal: 1280/720 }`) — the 640x480 default is too coarse to resolve barcode bars.
- Barcode decode constants (tuned against real failures): formats restricted to `EAN_13`/`EAN_8`/`UPC_A`/`UPC_E` via `DecodeHintType.POSSIBLE_FORMATS`, `TRY_HARDER` enabled, `delayBetweenScanAttempts: 100` (down from the 500ms default). Leaving formats unrestricted makes ZXing try QR/Aztec/PDF417/Data Matrix every frame, wasting most of the per-frame budget.

**Bundle size (Milestone 6):**
- Route-level `React.lazy` + `Suspense` for any screen carrying a camera/vision dependency. `WorkoutSession` (MediaPipe) and `BarcodeScanner` (ZXing) are both split out; together they were ~68% of the initial bundle and most visits open neither.
- Read the actual per-chunk build output before deciding what to split. The intuition that MediaPipe was the heaviest dependency was wrong by more than 3x — ZXing ships 482 KB of JS while MediaPipe ships 139 KB, because MediaPipe fetches its WASM and model at runtime rather than bundling them.

**Debug affordances:**
- Gate developer-only UI behind a `?debug=1` query param, NOT `import.meta.env.DEV`. Anything that depends on device hardware or a secure context can only be exercised in the production build, so a dev-only gate hides the tool in the exact environment it exists to diagnose.
- Keep high-frequency counters (decode attempts, frame stats) in a ref and flush to state on a timer — never `setState` from a loop running many times per second.
